A||||15||fierospeeder||Stupid Car advice given to you||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||11-21-2001||01:05 AM||||whats the dumbest thing someone said about your car, or how to fix it. etc...<BR>I seen on some other forums people say the dumbest thing to someone else's probs.<P><BR>I remember when i bought a vw beetle which didn't start.  My dad says "it needs a tuneup"  I knew crap about cars, except some car things from my magazines.   I put in a new starter and the buggy came alive.  BUT it was way to rusted to drive.  <P>moral of the story, if you dont hear the engine turning, then a tuneup isn't going to help much in getting it started.  PVC valve is bad. etc..<P><BR>my car stops faster when i hold the brake down to come to a skidding stop.<P>My car drives better in the snow then in the rain.  Its cause i can see better in the snow then when its raining.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||11-21-2001||10:13 AM||||The dumbest thing I have seen replied to a post was a recommendation to put automatic tranny fluid into a manual's clutch.  It'll destroy all the rubber parts in a manual, rather bad idea.<P>My worst related experience tends to be garages that think I am dumb and try to pass stuff off as fixed, or needing fixed.  Two good examples:<P>1) Took the car to a Pontiac dealer to get an engine code looked at.  I had actually pulled it (32-EGR system) and simply could not find the leak.  I told them I wanted it looked at.  They called, said the coil and wires were bad and arcing causing my problems!  I thought it was odd since both were about a month old!  Told them to leave them and I'd return them myself since they would still be under warrenty if bad, athough I knew they were fine.  I was even getting the code before their replacement anyway.  Picked the car up and it was running terrle.  They gave me what they called "quality used wires" which were arcing, and put a hole in the wire terminal boot that goes onto the coil to make the coil look bad.  Must have thought I was stupid enough to believe a car I delivered which ran fine but had an engine light would barely run with their used wires after I picked it up!  <P>2) I recently tried to buy a set of studded snow tires for my daily driver.  when I got home I was checkin out the studs, and noticed there weren't any in the inner trad blocks (there were holes for them) and about half of the ones in the outter tread blocks were half out of the holes and many of those were at 45 degree angles to the tread blcoks.  Gee, I don't think thats right!  I am still dealing with them!  They have the tires right now, and are waiting for a replacement set of four.  I told them not to stud the new set (I fear what they will do!!).  Not sure if I'll end up getting them studded or not since I do mostly thruway driving.  It sure is LOUD wth those things!<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||11-21-2001||11:14 AM||||Didn't happen to me, but my father was involved in it.  Back in the mid 50's my uncle Jim had one of those little Renaults with the exhaust system the diameter of your thumb.  His muffler went bad and he didn't have the money to replace it.  Pop tells him to cut a piece of garden hose and clamp it where the muffler was.  Jim does this and goes about three blocks before the hose melts and starts to stink.  His car smelt for three months.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever3000||11-21-2001||04:18 PM||||When I needed to rebuild the 289 engine of my 66 Mustang (see... I really do like Mustangs), it was to be my first engine rebuild.  My four-wheeling buddy had rebuilt Chevies before, and we were doing this in his garage.  He told me the intake manifold was aftermarket and not original, somebody must have had a 2-barrel and wanted this 4-barrel.  I wanted everything to be factory specs, so we traded with someone for a Ford 2-barrel intake manifold.   Much later I realized what made my friend think it wasn't stock - he didn't know the other names that Ford uses, like FoMoCo and AutoCraft etc!  The 4-barrel was original after all!  Oh well, people make mistakes.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||11-24-2001||09:23 AM||||I think I can 'win' this one:<P> My first car was a '68VW squareback. When I bought it there was an exhaust leak, a main bearing seal leak, and if you rev'd it to about 3k the engine didnt sould right, a bit of a rattel/clatter.<P> I had the car for a year and I drove it short distances (14 miles) to college and babied it - never rev'd the engine very high and (I cant believe I did this part) if I had to go 55mph I would wind it up to about 60, push the clutch in, let it coast down to about 50, then wind it back up - cause if you held it steady at 55 the clatter sounding like the engine was in pain.<P> A friend of mine kept telling me I was driving wrong, and that I was lugging the engine - he had a ford 500 station wagon with a V8. Finally one day I let him drive my VW for a bit, and he said, you got to rev it higher before you shift, and he showed me.<P> He showed me ok - about 5 miles down the road the rod bearing let go and Mr Piston had sex with Mrs Head (they mated).<P> In all fairness the engine did have bad rod and main bearings - and he was right, a VW needs to be reved higher - your shouldnt lug it around below 2,000 RPM - but being in tune with my sickly car I had managed to nurse it along in that condition for a year - and he blew the engine in about 10 minutes.||||reg||11
Z||000005||Oreif||11-24-2001||09:44 AM||||My Mom's Toyota was shifting wrong (auto) Took it to the dealer. They had the car for 3 days. Kept saying there was an electrical problem with the trans. Finally after giving us the run-around, Dad and I went to go pick up the car. When we get there we are told the aftermarket alarm system fried out the electronic control of the trans!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"><BR>After talking with the mechanic and service advisor, They NEVER looked at the trans! They were trying to figure out why with the doors closed if you turn on the ignition the doors lock. (This is a feature of the alarm system) They couldn't figure it out for 3 days!!! When we picked up the car, They had cut the power lock wires AND power window wires in the drivers door in trying to locate the problem!<BR>We called Toyota's regional office and they directed us to another dealer. We explained what happened. They were able to repair the wires and replaced the Toyota version of a TCC switch in the trans. We dropped it off at 5pm and they had it fixed by noon the next day.<P><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fogglethorpe||11-24-2001||12:19 PM||||"Don't worry about changing the oil...it just leaks out again. Keep it topped off."||||reg||1
